(ns kotoba.compiler.accelerator-variants
  "Total, deterministic per-vendor kernel variant selection for the ADR-0002
  accelerator KIR (ADR-0356; the NVIDIA CUDA-route row is JUN-57, the
  Intel and ARM rows are JUN-59/JUN-60 and share one
  subgroup-size-parametric emission, and the Apple row JUN-58 (ADR-0360)
  and the NVIDIA Vulkan-route row JUN-57 (ADR-0361) share that same
  emission on the Vulkan route -- MoltenVK is Apple's only EXECUTION route
  there, and NVIDIA's CUDA row has no execution path in this repository).
  The AMD row (JUN-84 / AWAI-40, ADR-0364) shares it too, on the Vulkan
  route (RADV) and the WGSL route (:amd's vendor-default target) -- its
  RDNA wave32/wave64 duality is answered by the fold's runtime width
  query, never at selection time.
  The Apple row also rides the MSL route since ADR-0356's and ADR-0360's
  2026-10-23 amendments (JUN-58's own decision), and the NVIDIA row rides
  the CUDA route since ADR-0361's same-day amendment (JUN-57's own
  decision) -- as the SECOND rung of that route's ladder, under
  :warp-shuffle-reduce's precedence.
  UNMEASURED, almost all of it. Two variant reductions have EXECUTED on
  the vendor hardware a row names, both on Apple M1 Max through
  measurement harnesses: :subgroup-reduce (GLSL arm via wire-42/MoltenVK,
  PR #1347/#1348, 2026-10-19; MSL arm via a native Metal harness,
  2026-10-23, at W = 32..1024), losing to :baseline in every measured
  cell but W = 32 -- the one mixed cell, whose signs are scoped to the
  slow clock regime and whose answer is a decision (see apple-w32-note)
  -- and :simdgroup-reduce (the same native-Metal channel, 2026-10-23,
  at W = 256), BEATING :baseline there (paired median ratios
  0.790/0.800/0.799 for :sum/:max/:min, claim-protocol rule 2
  REGIME-INVARIANT FASTER, 18/18 per-run ratios below 1.0). No other
  vendor's hardware has run a variant, and no variant has an execution
  path in the product runtime: the wire-42 (:gpu/compute) route has no
  producer today -- the C loader brokers were removed (main 36479076) and
  the replacement is ADR 0371's GPU provider process, not implemented.
  Every answer is a design choice marked :measured? false except the
  measured cells ADR-0360 question (1)'s 2026-10-23 decision unlocks (JUN-58):
  the narrowed :baseline answers of the measured device (Apple M1 Max,
  reported subgroup size 32) on the MSL route at W = 64/128/256/512/1024 --
  the cells whose refutation ran the emitters' own bytes on hardware under
  the sweep protocol -- and, where the fold's own rule-6 fact is reported,
  the :simdgroup-reduce answer at W = 128/256/512/1024 (this variant's
  OWN
  flip cells, measured faster than :baseline 2026-10-23/24,
  apple-simdgroup-flip-note).
  Those answers carry :measured? true and a
  :measured-cell record naming the tested device class, the channel and the
  evidence. The
  W = 32 cell ran under the same channel and keeps the lock: its verdict is
  mixed and regime-scoped, and its :baseline answer is the low-band
  decision's, not a measurement's. No
  other answer may be reported as optimization evidence until a hardware
  measurement replaces its basis.

  The Metal-native :simdgroup-reduce row (ADR-0373, rule 3 step 2 landed
  2026-10-23) is SELECTABLE under the MSL ladder: outside the carved-out
  measured class, an Apple MSL descriptor reporting the fold's own rule-6
  fact (:simd-reduce -- the device's SIMD-group exposes simd_sum/simd_max/
  simd_min) and a reported SIMD-group width dividing W answers
  :simdgroup-reduce FIRST (the vendor-native rung); a descriptor reporting
  the shuffle fold's facts but not :simd-reduce falls to :subgroup-reduce
  (the second rung, byte-for-byte the old answer); everything else answers
  :baseline. The measured-cell carve-out outranks rung 1 everywhere but
  this variant's OWN flip cells: the narrowed M1
  Max class keeps its reduce-shape answers (:baseline, measured where the
  flip keys hold) at every W but 128/256/512/1024, where this variant's
  own
  measurements (2026-10-23/24, apple-simdgroup-flip-note) flipped the
  cells -- with the fold's rule-6 fact reported, those cells answer
  :simdgroup-reduce, MEASURED faster than :baseline. Every other
  :simdgroup-reduce answer is UNMEASURED (:measured? false).

  The functions are TOTAL, like the dispatch table in
  kotoba.compiler.accelerator-targets: a vendor or kernel shape outside the
  table answers :baseline, never a throw. Selection time is the wrong place
  to refuse -- a portable kernel that runs is worth more than an error."
  {:kotoba/export [select-variant variant-bases variants]}
  (:require [kotoba.compiler.accelerator :as accelerator]
            [kotoba.compiler.accelerator-simdgroup-reduce :as simdgroup]
            [kotoba.compiler.accelerator-subgroup-reduce :as subgroup-reduce]
            [kotoba.lang.text :as str]))

(def variants
  "Every variant selection may answer. Backed by the emitter's admitted set --
  a variant that cannot be emitted is never selected. [Honesty mark
  2026-10-23, rule-3 step 2: :simdgroup-reduce is not only admitted (step 1,
  MSL route) but SELECTABLE now -- select-variant answers it under ADR-0373's
  MSL ladder; the admitted set remains a NECESSARY gate, not a promise that
  every member is selectable on every route.]"
  accelerator/kernel-variants)

(def min-warp-shuffle-workgroup
  "The smallest workgroup the shuffle variant admits: one full NVIDIA warp.
  Below this the variant would need partial-warp masking and gets :baseline."
  32)

(def ^:private route-names
  "One emission route under the two names callers may hold for it: the api
  name and the emitter target name. ADR-0359 decision 3 is why :vulkan and
  :glsl-v1 name one route (the api :vulkan opens compiled SPIR-V and the
  only source-to-SPIR-V path this repository owns is glslang from GLSL);
  :cuda and :cuda-v1 are the CUDA route's two spellings the same way,
  :metal and :msl-v1 are the MSL route's, and :webgpu and :wgsl-v1 are the
  WGSL route's (ADR-0363). The sets are
  disjoint, so at most one route answers per target keyword and the lookup
  is order-independent."
  {:cuda-v1 #{:cuda :cuda-v1}
   :glsl-v1 #{:vulkan :glsl-v1}
   :msl-v1 #{:metal :msl-v1}
   :wgsl-v1 #{:webgpu :wgsl-v1}})

(defn- emitter-route
  "The emission route a target keyword names, or nil when it names none.
  TOTAL: an unknown or absent target answers nil and selection then falls
  back to :baseline rather than guessing an emitter."
  [target]
  (some (fn [[route names]] (when (contains? names target) route)) route-names))

(def ^:private apple-measured-fragment
  "The measured-device fragment of the ONE device where the Apple row has
  hardware measurements: \"Apple M1 Max\" (the GLSL arm via wire-42/MoltenVK
  2026-10-19, PR #1347/#1348, and the MSL arm via a native Metal harness
  2026-10-23, ADR-0360's measurement amendment). The uB5G idiom from
  kotoba.compiler.accelerator-targets: evidence about a NAMED device acts
  on that device's name -- case-insensitively, per name part -- and never
  past it. Coarse on purpose (a name carrying the fragment counts as the
  measured device)."
  "m1 max")

(defn- device-name-carries?
  "Whether any name part the descriptor carries (:device, :name, or the
  :vendor name key -- the same three keys uB5G reads in
  kotoba.compiler.accelerator-targets/recommend) carries `fragment`,
  case-insensitively. An unnamed (vendor-id-only) descriptor answers
  false: no name, no evidence to act on."
  [dev fragment]
  (boolean (some (fn [part]
                   (and (string? part)
                        (not (str/blank? part))
                        (str/includes? (str/lower part) fragment)))
                 [(:device dev) (:name dev) (:vendor dev)])))

(def ^:private apple-measured-channel
  "The channel the contract-satisfying measurements ran through: the
  native Metal harness (scripts/gpu-subgroup-reduce-metal-measure.{m,py,
  cljk}) dispatching the emitters' own MSL bytes. ADR-0360 question (1)'s
  2026-10-23 decision admits this channel for the MSL arm: wire-42
  executes SPIR-V through Vulkan and can never run MSL, so a wire-42-only
  clause would be a deadlock for that arm, not an honesty lock."
  :native-metal)

(def ^:private apple-measured-workgroups
  "The workgroup widths whose measurement CHOSE the answer the flip marks:
  W = 256 (ADR-0360's measurement amendment, 2026-10-23), W = 512/1024
  (the same day's W-sweep) and W = 64/128 (the same day's low band -- all
  three operators 1.20-1.25, the same penalty band as W >= 256). W = 32
  ran under the same channel and is MEASURED, but it is NOT in this set:
  its verdict is mixed and regime-scoped -- the low band saw :sum
  0.916-0.920 with every one of those runs in the slow clock regime, and
  the later shape-2 and op-order cells
  (docs/evidence/gpu-subgroup-reduce-m1max-shape2-20261023,
  docs/evidence/gpu-subgroup-reduce-m1max-oporder-20261023) measured the
  scoping itself: the sign follows the clock level for all three operators
  (op-order within-pair medians, slow vs fast: :sum 0.919 vs 1.018, :max
  0.917 vs 1.029, :min 0.920 vs 1.018) -- and it published no
  regime-robust verdict that chose an answer -- the W = 32
  answer is the low-band decision's (apple-w32-note) and keeps the lock.
  The GLSL arm never flips at any
  width (its runs are LABELED BOOTSTRAP, see the honesty-lock prose)."
  #{64 128 256 512 1024})

(def ^:private apple-measured-subgroup-size
  "The reported subgroup size the tested device gave: 32
  ([[threads_per_simdgroup]] on Apple M1 Max, confirming the Vulkan-side
  reading). The measurement contract keys the flip to the tested device's
  name AND its reported subgroup size -- a descriptor reporting anything
  else is not the tested device class."
  32)

(def ^:private apple-measured-shapes
  "The element counts the contract-satisfying cells ran: the flip-contract
  WIDENING DECISION (ADR-0360's third amendment, 2026-10-23, JUN-58) -- the
  key is no longer the single shape n = 1,000,003 but the two MEASURED
  shapes, n = 1,000,003 (every shape-1 cell) and n = 2^20 (the shape-2
  records, gpu-subgroup-reduce-m1max-shape2-20261023 and
  gpu-subgroup-reduce-m1max-shape2-band-20261023). Shape 2's completed
  flip-set band measured the variant slower in all 15 width x operator
  cells (1.166-1.239, paired within-run medians) -- the same sign and the
  same penalty band as shape 1 at every width -- so it is a contract cell,
  not corroboration. The selection answer cannot vary with n; the record
  names the shapes so no claim extends past these two -- shapes beyond
  them are UNMEASURED."
  [1000003 1048576])

(def ^:private apple-measured-evidence
  "The evidence directories of the contract-satisfying cells: the W = 256
  measurement, the W-sweep (W = 256 anchor / 512 / 1024) and the low band
  (W = 32 / 64 / 128) at shape 1, and the shape-2 records (W = 256/32 and
  the flip-set band W = 64/128/512/1024) the widening decision added."
  ["docs/evidence/gpu-subgroup-reduce-m1max-20261023"
   "docs/evidence/gpu-subgroup-reduce-m1max-wsweep-20261023"
   "docs/evidence/gpu-subgroup-reduce-m1max-wsweep-low-20261023"
   "docs/evidence/gpu-subgroup-reduce-m1max-shape2-20261023"
   "docs/evidence/gpu-subgroup-reduce-m1max-shape2-band-20261023"])

(def ^:private apple-narrow-note
  "APPLE ROW NARROWED (ADR-0360's second decision, 2026-10-23, JUN-58):
  this named device carries the measured-device fragment \"m1 max\", where
  hardware measured :subgroup-reduce SLOWER than :baseline -- the MSL arm
  via a native Metal harness 2026-10-23 (ADR-0360's measurement amendment:
  ~22% slower at W = 256 across :sum/:max/:min; the same day's W-sweep:
  still slower at W = 512 and W = 1024, paired median ratios 1.19-1.20 and
  1.18-1.19 -- the contract-satisfying cells of question (1)'s decision)
  and the GLSL arm via wire-42/MoltenVK 2026-10-19 (PR #1347/#1348:
  ~20-27% slower at W = 256, never faster over W = 32..256 -- the same
  sign, but LABELED BOOTSTRAP: an unbalanced replay order biases the ratio
  and its own record publishes no verdict). The refuted variant is not
  selected on the device that refuted it; every other Apple device keeps
  the designed row (UNMEASURED). The low band completed the MSL band
  W = 32..1024 on this device: W = 64..1024 rests on the device's own
  measurements and those cells flip the honesty lock; W = 32 is the one
  mixed cell and its answer carries its own decision note (apple-w32-note).
  Corroboration (2026-10-23, no flag movement): the demotion basis
  replicates at a second shape -- n = 2^20
  (docs/evidence/gpu-subgroup-reduce-m1max-shape2-20261023), the padded
  tail gone, W = 256 penalty 1.220-1.227 against shape 1's 1.217-1.220 --
  so it is not a padded-tail artifact. The flip contract WIDENED on
  2026-10-23 (the widening decision, ADR-0360's third amendment): shape 2's
  completed flip-set band
  (docs/evidence/gpu-subgroup-reduce-m1max-shape2-band-20261023) measured
  the variant slower in all 15 width x operator
  cells (1.166-1.239, paired within-run medians) -- the same sign and the
  same penalty band as shape 1 at every width -- so both measured shapes
  are contract cells now (the record's :shapes names them) and shapes
  beyond these two stay UNMEASURED.")

(def ^:private apple-w32-note
  "W = 32, THE DECIDED CELL (ADR-0360's low-band decision, 2026-10-23,
  JUN-58): the low-band measurement
  (docs/evidence/gpu-subgroup-reduce-m1max-wsweep-low-20261023) measured
  this cell and it is the one where the variant is not slower -- :sum
  0.918 (0.916-0.920 in every fresh process), :max 0.923, :min 1.019 --
  but the signs are scoped to the slow clock regime the device sat in
  while every :sum run of that cell happened (per-run median gpu-ns 109k
  -> 36k mid-session). The scoping is now MEASURED, not inferred: the
  shape-2 cell (docs/evidence/gpu-subgroup-reduce-m1max-shape2-20261023,
  n = 2^20) replicated the straddle (:sum 0.919 / :max 0.921 / :min 0.918
  overall, fast-level :max 1.026 and :min 1.021-1.026 against slow-level
  ~0.92), and the op-order cell
  (docs/evidence/gpu-subgroup-reduce-m1max-oporder-20261023) built the
  instrument the shape-2 record named as the structural blocker (sum
  dispatched last) and sampled :sum at the fast level for the first time:
  within-pair medians by clock level -- :sum slow 0.919 / middle 0.932 /
  fast 1.018, :max slow 0.917 / fast 1.029, :min slow 0.920 / fast 1.018
  -- so the sign follows the regime for all three operators and the W = 32
  win is a slow-regime artifact in each of them. A
  regime-scoped sign is not a selection basis -- the same standard that
  keeps the LABELED BOOTSTRAP GLSL runs verdict-free -- and an
  operator-split answer at one cell off the two measured shapes would be
  overfitting, so this cell keeps answering :baseline for all three
  operators. The
  demotion here is a DECISION on measured ground, not the fail-safe it was
  before the low band ran, and the answer keeps the honesty lock
  (:measured? false): no regime-robust verdict chose it -- every cell that
  sampled both clock levels straddles 1 across them -- the decision did.")

(def ^:private apple-simdgroup-carveout-note
  "THE MEASURED-CELL CARVE-OUT (ADR-0373, rule 3 step 2, 2026-10-23,
  JUN-58): this descriptor reports the :simdgroup-reduce row's rule-6 fact
  (:simd-reduce) and the row's shape holds, but it names the measured
  device class (\"m1 max\"), whose reduce-shape answers are measured or
  decided and CLOSED to design rows: measurement beats design, so
  :simdgroup-reduce does not answer here at any W but its own flip cells
  (W = 128, W = 256, W = 512 and W = 1024 -- each flipped when this
  variant's
  own measurement ran there: W = 256 and W = 512 on 2026-10-23, W = 1024
  and W = 128
  on 2026-10-24, see apple-simdgroup-flip-note; at every
  other W this note is what the cell carries). The class keeps :baseline
  at every other W (W = 64 where :subgroup-reduce was measured slower;
  W = 32 by the low-band decision). A :measured? true flag on any
  cell this note carries marks the :subgroup-reduce refutation ONLY --
  its :measured-cell record's :evidence names those runs and says nothing
  about this variant; where the flip's keys do not hold the answer is the
  carve-out's (:measured? false), not a measurement's. The M1 Max
  measurements that refuted :subgroup-reduce neither condemn nor bless
  this variant (uB5G, reverse reading) -- only the W = 128, W = 256,
  W = 512 and
  W = 1024 runs of THIS variant measured it, and only there does a
  measured :simdgroup-reduce answer exist.")

(def ^:private apple-simdgroup-measured-workgroups
  "The workgroup widths whose measurement of THIS variant CHOSE the
  answer the flip marks: W = 128, W = 256, W = 512 and W = 1024 (the
  honesty-lock
  flip anchors -- the cells the native-Metal measurement of :baseline vs
  :simdgroup-reduce ran, 2026-10-23/24, ADR-0373's flip contract applied
  to this variant's own cells; W = 512, W = 1024 and W = 128 are the
  W-sweep
  series' second, third and fourth cells).
  Every other width is UNMEASURED for this
  variant: W = 32/64 ran for :subgroup-reduce only and say
  nothing about this variant (uB5G, reverse reading)."
  #{128 256 512 1024})

(def ^:private apple-simdgroup-measured-shapes
  "The element counts this variant's contract-satisfying cells ran:
  n = 1,000,003 (the flip contract's ground shape) ONLY, at each flipped
  W (128, 256, 512, 1024). Shape 2
  (n = 2^20) is UNMEASURED for this variant at every cell. The selection
  answer cannot vary with n, and the record's :shapes names the measured
  shape so no claim extends past it."
  [1000003])

(def ^:private apple-simdgroup-measured-evidence
  "The evidence directory of each flipped cell's contract-satisfying
  runs, keyed by workgroup width -- each flip cell's :measured-cell
  record names ITS OWN runs (the :subgroup-reduce runs say nothing about
  this variant, uB5G, reverse reading): W = 256 -> the step-3
  native-Metal measurement of :baseline vs :simdgroup-reduce; W = 512,
  W = 1024 and
  W = 128 -> the W-sweep series' second, third and fourth cells
  (summary.md,
  run-log.txt, compile-log.txt, shaders/ in each)."
  {256 ["docs/evidence/gpu-simdgroup-reduce-m1max-20261023"]
   512 ["docs/evidence/gpu-simdgroup-reduce-m1max-wsweep-20261023/w512"]
   1024 ["docs/evidence/gpu-simdgroup-reduce-m1max-wsweep-20261023/w1024"]
   128 ["docs/evidence/gpu-simdgroup-reduce-m1max-wsweep-20261023/w128"]})

(def ^:private apple-simdgroup-flip-note
  "THIS VARIANT'S OWN FLIP CELLS (ADR-0373's flip contract applied to
  :simdgroup-reduce's own measured cells, 2026-10-23/24, JUN-58): the
  conforming channel (ADR-0360 question (1)'s native-Metal harness
  family, the emitters' own bytes) measured :baseline vs
  :simdgroup-reduce at FOUR cells -- W = 256 (the honesty-lock flip
  anchor) and W = 512, W = 1024 and W = 128 (the W-sweep series' second,
  third and fourth
  cells), all
  n = 1000003 (the flip contract's ground shape) -- on the
  tested device class (name fragment \"m1 max\", reported subgroup size
  32), the historical protocol unchanged (6 fresh processes, 20
  interleaved AB/BA pairs per operator per run, 2 warmups discarded,
  verified read-back). Paired median ratios (:simdgroup-reduce /
  :baseline gpu ns): W = 256 -- :sum 0.790, :max 0.800, :min 0.799;
  W = 512 -- :sum 0.815, :max 0.823, :min 0.824;
  W = 1024 -- :sum 0.840, :max 0.847, :min 0.850;
  W = 128 -- :sum 0.775, :max 0.789, :min 0.789 -- the OPPOSITE
  sign to :subgroup-reduce at the identical cells (~1.22 at W = 256,
  1.196/1.195/1.197 at W = 512, 1.186/1.186/1.185 at W = 1024,
  1.244/1.243/1.234 at W = 128) -- and at
  all four cells the
  claim-protocol rule-2 verdict is REGIME-INVARIANT FASTER (18/18
  per-run ratios below 1.0 at each cell, every observed regime
  one-sided), so each cell is a selection basis. Measurement beats the
  carve-out's earlier decision exactly here: the cells answer
  :simdgroup-reduce with
  :measured? true. WHAT THE NUMBERS DO NOT LICENSE: every other W
  (32/64) keeps the carve-out's :baseline; shape 2
  (n = 2^20) and every shape beyond n = 1000003 are UNMEASURED at these
  cells (selection cannot vary with n; the record's :shapes names the
  measured shape); the GLSL arm, every other Apple device, every other
  vendor and cross-day replication are UNMEASURED. Read-back: 36/36
  ok=1 per cell, :max/:min bit-exact vs the CPU reference and between
  arms, :sum 3.05e-05 between arms at W = 256 and W = 512, 6.10e-05
  at W = 1024 and 1.53e-05 at W = 128 (regrouping). Cross-operator level
  identity is REFUTED at all four cells (14/15 run pairs diverge at
  W = 256, 11/15 at W = 512, 9/15 at W = 1024, 12/15 at W = 128 -- data,
  not a claim):
  per-operator level names stay operator-scoped. Evidence:
  docs/evidence/gpu-simdgroup-reduce-m1max-20261023 (W = 256),
  docs/evidence/gpu-simdgroup-reduce-m1max-wsweep-20261023/w512 (W = 512),
  docs/evidence/gpu-simdgroup-reduce-m1max-wsweep-20261023/w1024 (W = 1024),
  docs/evidence/gpu-simdgroup-reduce-m1max-wsweep-20261023/w128 (W = 128).")

(def variant-bases
  "Why each variant exists. Design rationale, not measurement evidence."
  {:baseline
   "ADR-0002 v1 tree reduction over workgroup shared memory; the portable
    fallback. Execution record (reconciled 2026-10-23): NO variant has an
    execution path in this repository today -- the wire-42 route died with
    the C loader and ADR 0371's provider is the replacement track -- and
    :subgroup-reduce has EXECUTED on Apple M1 Max through measurement
    harnesses (GLSL arm via wire-42/MoltenVK, PR #1347/#1348, 2026-10-19;
    MSL arm via a native Metal harness, ADR-0360's measurement amendment,
    2026-10-23), losing to :baseline in both arms, and
    :simdgroup-reduce has EXECUTED on the same device through the
    native-Metal harness (2026-10-23/24, W = 128/256/512/1024), BEATING
    :baseline at all four cells (paired median ratios
    0.775/0.789/0.789 at W = 128,
    0.790/0.800/0.799 at W = 256, 0.815/0.823/0.824 at W = 512 and
    0.840/0.847/0.850 at W = 1024 for :sum/:max/:min, claim-protocol
    rule 2 REGIME-INVARIANT FASTER, 18/18 per-run ratios below 1.0 at
    each)."
   :warp-shuffle-reduce
   "NVIDIA (sm_70+) warp-shuffle reduce: a per-warp __shfl_down_sync fold,
    then one cross-warp stage through shared memory -- one barrier instead
    of the baseline's log2(W) cascade. The design assumption (UNMEASURED) is
    that removing the barrier cascade wins on NVIDIA hardware. It replaces no
    workgroup row and changes no reduction semantics: the same elements are
    grouped in a different order (f32 :sum may differ in the low bits;
    :max/:min are order-independent)."
   :subgroup-reduce
   "Intel (JUN-59, ADR-0356), ARM (JUN-60, ADR-0357), Apple (JUN-58,
    ADR-0360), NVIDIA (JUN-57, ADR-0361 -- the Vulkan route, and the
    CUDA route as the ladder's second rung under :warp-shuffle-reduce's
    precedence) and AMD (JUN-84, ADR-0364 -- the Vulkan and WGSL routes)
    subgroup reduce: an
    intra-subgroup fold whose width is queried at RUNTIME (gl_SubgroupSize)
    and never baked in -- Intel subgroup size is device-chosen among
    8/16/32, Mali's spans 4..32, Apple's SIMD-group is 32 and RDNA's wave
    is 32 or 64 as the shader chooses, so a baked
    width would be wrong somewhere. The
    fold needs no barriers and no shared memory; one write barrier plus a
    cross-subgroup tree over the W/S partials replaces the baseline
    log2(W)-stage cascade (4/3/2 stages at W = 128 for S = 8/16/32 against
    the baseline's 7 -- stage arithmetic, not a speed claim). The design
    assumption (UNMEASURED) is that removing most of the barrier cascade
    wins on Intel, Mali, Apple and NVIDIA hardware. The assumption is
    UNMEASURED on Intel, Mali and NVIDIA (no hardware there has run the
    variant) and MEASURED-CONTRADICTED on Apple: Apple M1 Max ran the
    variant twice and it lost to :baseline both times (GLSL arm via
    wire-42/MoltenVK, PR #1347/#1348, 2026-10-19: ~20-27% slower at W=256
    and never faster over W=32..256; MSL arm via a native Metal harness,
    ADR-0360's measurement amendment, 2026-10-23: ~22% slower at W=256
    across :sum/:max/:min, and the same day's native-Metal W-sweep: still
    slower at W=512 and W=1024, paired median ratios 1.19-1.20 and
    1.18-1.19) -- one device and narrow shape coverage. The
    measured cell DECIDED the row's fate on 2026-10-23 (ADR-0360's second
    decision): the row is NARROWED to the measured device -- a descriptor
    naming the measured device answers :baseline, because that device
    measured the variant slower -- and stands (UNMEASURED) on every other
    device. It
    replaces no workgroup row. Semantics, scoped as ADR-0357 P1 requires:
    the reduction groups the same admitted elements as the :baseline tree
    in a different order -- f32 :sum agrees with the baseline to low bits on
    well-conditioned inputs, and on cancellation-sensitive inputs the
    divergence is NOT confined to the low bits and stays an UNMEASURED
    divergence regime from the baseline; :max/:min are order-independent
    within the ordinary-value domain only (no NaN payloads, no negative
    zeros -- ADR-0356 rule 2)."
   :simdgroup-reduce
   "Apple (JUN-58, ADR-0373) Metal-native SIMD-group reduce: the reduce
    shape on the MSL route ONLY, whose intra-SIMD-group fold is ONE Metal
    SIMD-group reduction intrinsic per operator (simd_sum / simd_max /
    simd_min) instead of the shuffle ladder -- everything above it is the
    shared fold, byte-for-byte :subgroup-reduce's MSL arm (the partials
    array, the one write barrier, the cross-SIMD-group tree). It is a
    SEPARATE variant, never a rewrite of :subgroup-reduce. Selection is
    the MSL ladder (rule 3 step 2): the vendor-native FIRST rung for an
    Apple MSL descriptor reporting the fold's own rule-6 fact
    (:simd-reduce -- the device's SIMD-group exposes the reduction
    intrinsics) and a reported SIMD-group width dividing W;
    :basic/:shuffle-relative are the SHUFFLE fold's facts and are not
    required here (the tree uses threadgroup barriers). The measured-cell
    carve-out outranks it everywhere but this row's OWN flip cells: at
    W = 128, W = 256, W = 512 and W = 1024 on the measured class (Apple
    M1 Max,
    reported subgroup size
    32, MSL route, n = 1000003) the native-Metal measurements of
    2026-10-23/24 measured this row FASTER than :baseline (paired median
    ratios 0.775/0.789/0.789 at W = 128,
    0.790/0.800/0.799 at W = 256, 0.815/0.823/0.824 at W = 512
    and 0.840/0.847/0.850 at W = 1024
    for :sum/:max/:min, claim-protocol rule 2
    REGIME-INVARIANT FASTER at all four cells) and the flip contract
    answers it there,
    MEASURED (apple-simdgroup-flip-note); the class keeps :baseline at
    every other W and every other cell stays closed to design rows. The
    design assumption (UNMEASURED) is that one reduction intrinsic beats
    the shuffle ladder anywhere else -- four cells, one shape
    (n = 1000003) and one device are measured; the shuffle fold's own rationale
    measured CONTRADICTED on that device (M1 Max); that refutation
    neither condemns nor blesses this row outside the flip cells. No
    speed claim outside the flip cells' own recorded scope. Semantics:
    :sum is scoped to low bits (the intrinsic's association order is the
    vendor's own, ADR-0357 P1 option b); :max/:min are bit-for-bit within
    the ordinary-value domain only (non-NaN payloads, no negative zeros).
    It replaces no workgroup row."})

(defn select-variant
  "The kernel variant for a (vendor, kir) pair, optionally scoped to the
  emission `target` and the `device` descriptor the dispatch layers carry.
  TOTAL and deterministic: the same arguments answer the same map, forever.

  The rows (ADR-0356 as amended by ADR-0357; every answer carries
  :measured? false except the measured cells of the Apple row's narrowing
  on the MSL route -- ADR-0360 question (1)'s decision, tested in
  the-measured-cells-flip-the-honesty-lock):

  - :warp-shuffle-reduce -- NVIDIA reduction kernels over :sum/:max/:min
    whose workgroup reaches one full warp, on the CUDA route (:cuda or
    :cuda-v1), on a device confirming compute capability 7.0+ (rule 6: the
    sm_70+ floor in variant-bases is a real requirement; a descriptor that
    does not confirm it answers :baseline, fail closed).

  - :subgroup-reduce -- Intel (JUN-59), ARM (JUN-60), Apple (JUN-58,
    ADR-0360), NVIDIA on the Vulkan route (JUN-57, ADR-0361) and AMD
    (JUN-84, ADR-0364) reduction
    kernels
    over :sum/:max/:min with a workgroup of at least one subgroup floor
    (the emission's own predicate, kotoba.compiler.accelerator-subgroup-
    reduce/applicable?), on the Vulkan route (:vulkan or :glsl-v1) for all
    five vendors, for Intel, ARM and AMD (ADR-0363; for :amd ADR-0364)
    on the WGSL route
    (:webgpu or :wgsl-v1 -- the vendor-default target for all three), for
    Apple ONLY (ADR-0360's 2026-10-23 amendment, JUN-58's own decision) on
    the MSL route (:metal or :msl-v1 -- Apple's vendor-default target),
    and for NVIDIA (ADR-0361's same-day amendment, JUN-57's own decision)
    on the CUDA route (:cuda or :cuda-v1) as the SECOND rung of that
    route's ladder: :warp-shuffle-reduce is checked first and keeps
    precedence whenever its gate holds (a CONFIRMED compute capability
    7.0+ and W >= 32) -- every answer it gives is unchanged -- and this
    row answers only where the warp row's gate does not reach: a
    descriptor that reports the rule-6 subgroup facts and NO compute
    capability gets the parametric fold instead of :baseline (the same
    treatment the Vulkan row already gives an absent capability). The
    ladder is total and deterministic: :warp-shuffle-reduce ->
    :subgroup-reduce -> :baseline. The two rungs spell the same fold (the
    CUDA arm queries the width at runtime as warpSize where the warp row
    bakes 32), so the choice changes no reduction semantics -- it is a
    capability-evidence question, not an algorithmic one. Under
    ADR-0356 rule 6 and ADR-0357's capability precondition: the device
    descriptor must report, for the compute stage, the subgroup operations
    the fold needs (:subgroup-ops containing :basic and :shuffle-relative)
    and a subgroup size (:subgroup-size) that DIVIDES the workgroup -- which
    also refuses a subgroup larger than the workgroup, as rule 6 demands.
    The NVIDIA row carries the CUDA row's sm_70+ floor on top: whenever the
    descriptor reports a compute capability at all, one below 7.0 (or a
    malformed one) answers :baseline, fail closed; an absent capability is
    not a refusal, the reported subgroup facts are the capability evidence
    (ADR-0361).
    The reported width is taken as the device gives it (8/16/32 on Intel,
    4..32 on Mali, 32 on Apple's SIMD-groups, 32 or 64 as the shader
    chooses on RDNA, 32 -- the warp -- on NVIDIA
    Vulkan); the emission is parametric
    over it. The descriptor keys are the contract this table consumes and
    their producer DOES NOT EXIST YET: the wire-42 answers of the (today
    unimplemented) ADR-0371 GPU provider carry no subgroup facts (INFO is
    four fields, DEVCLASS one class word), so a probe or provider extension
    must report them -- the gap is named in ADR-0360 and the rows fail
    closed without them: a descriptor that reports none of them answers
    :baseline. The keys are the contract on EVERY route: the ADR-0362
    wire-42 SUBGROUP request is the Vulkan route's producer, and a
    Metal-native probe would fill the same keys directly from the device's
    SIMD-group facts (:basic / :shuffle-relative are this table's own
    capability vocabulary -- the two ops the fold needs -- not a Vulkan
    binding).

  - :simdgroup-reduce -- Apple (JUN-58, ADR-0373) Metal-native SIMD-group
    reduce, the reduce shape on the MSL route ONLY, and the MSL ladder's
    FIRST rung (rule 3 step 2): outside the carved-out measured class, an
    Apple MSL descriptor (:metal or :msl-v1) reporting the fold's own
    rule-6 fact (:simd-reduce in :subgroup-ops -- evidence the device's
    SIMD-group exposes simd_sum/simd_max/simd_min) and a :subgroup-size
    dividing W answers this variant; :basic/:shuffle-relative are the
    SHUFFLE fold's facts and are NOT required here (the cross-SIMD-group
    tree uses threadgroup barriers). A descriptor reporting the shuffle
    fold's facts but not :simd-reduce falls to :subgroup-reduce (the
    second rung, byte-for-byte the old answer); everything else answers
    :baseline (rule 1, total, no throw). The measured-cell carve-out
    outranks rung 1 everywhere but this variant's OWN flip cells: at
    W = 128, W = 256, W = 512 and W = 1024 on the measured class
    (\"m1 max\" AND
    reported subgroup size
    32) a descriptor reporting :simd-reduce answers THIS variant,
    MEASURED faster than :baseline (apple-simdgroup-flip-note) -- the only
    cells where this variant has hardware evidence. At every other W the
    carve-out stands: the class's reduce-shape answers are measured or
    decided and closed to design rows -- and the row is Apple's and the
    MSL route's alone. The fact's producer gap is ADR-0373's: the wire-42
    SUBGROUP vocabulary is ADR-0362's eight VkSubgroupFeatureFlagBits
    tokens and does NOT carry :simd-reduce (device-from-subgroup parses
    that vocabulary only), so a Metal-native probe or a provider extension
    must report the key -- the row fails closed without it. Every answer
    this row gives is UNMEASURED (:measured? false) except its own flip
    cells (W = 128, W = 256, W = 512 and W = 1024 on the measured class,
    MSL
    route,
    n = 1000003 -- the
    only cells hardware measured, and measured FASTER than :baseline).

  Everything else answers :baseline: other vendors (:unknown, :qualcomm
  and any name outside the five classified rows -- :amd has carried a
  designed row since ADR-0364), other targets, ewise kernels, sub-minimum
  workgroups, a malformed kernel (the COMPLETE KIR is validated first --
  shape checks alone would admit W=300 and worse), a device that confirms
  no capability, or no device at all.

  :measured? is the honesty lock: it flips only when hardware measurement
  (JUN-57 on NVIDIA, JUN-59 on Intel, JUN-60 on Mali, JUN-58 on Apple,
  JUN-84 on AMD) replaces the basis text of the row it stands on. A
  measurement can REFUTE a row's basis, not only validate it. ADR-0360's
  measurement-contract question (1) is DECIDED (2026-10-23, JUN-58): for
  the MSL arm a native Metal harness that dispatches the emitters' own
  bytes under the sweep protocol SATISFIES the contract -- wire-42 executes
  SPIR-V through Vulkan and can never run MSL, so the route clause was a
  deadlock for this arm rather than an honesty lock, and the properties it
  protects (the emitters' own bytes, the sweep protocol, verified
  read-back, a named device, a record under docs/evidence/) all hold. The
  flip is keyed per ANSWER CELL: the tested device class (the uB5G name
  fragment \"m1 max\" AND the reported subgroup size 32), the channel that
  measured the arm (:native-metal, the MSL route), and the measured
  workgroup widths (W = 64/128/256/512/1024 -- W = 32 measured too but
  DECIDED, not verdict-chosen; its answer keeps the lock, see
  apple-w32-note) -- plus this variant's OWN flip cells, the
  :simdgroup-reduce answers at W = 128, W = 256, W = 512 and W = 1024
  where the
  fold's rule-6 fact is
  reported (2026-10-23/24's measurements of this variant, faster than
  :baseline at all four cells, apple-simdgroup-flip-note). Exactly those
  cells carry
  :measured? true and a :measured-cell record; every other answer keeps
  false. The 2026-10-19 wire-42 baseline-vs-variant runs (PR #1347/#1348)
  are NOT verdict-grade: their own record (docs/evidence/gpu/loadsweep-
  20261019/README.md) labels them LABELED BOOTSTRAP -- an unbalanced replay
  order biases the ratio beyond drift -- and publishes no verdict, so the
  GLSL arm keeps :measured? false at every width; those runs corroborate
  the sign and nothing more.

  ADR-0360's second decision (2026-10-23, JUN-58) NARROWS the Apple row
  to the measured device: a descriptor whose name parts (:device, :name,
  or the :vendor name key) carry the measured-device fragment \"m1 max\"
  (case-insensitively -- the uB5G idiom of
  kotoba.compiler.accelerator-targets) answers :baseline for the reduce
  shape on BOTH Apple routes, annotated with the refutation in :basis.
  The narrowing acts exactly where the evidence is: every other Apple
  device -- including an unnamed {:vendor-id 4203} descriptor -- keeps
  the designed :subgroup-reduce row (UNMEASURED), and non-Apple vendors
  are untouched. A refuted device WITHOUT rule-6 facts is the ordinary
  fail-closed :baseline and carries no narrowing note (the note is only
  the row's own demotion). No report may
  cite an answer here as optimization evidence outside the flipped cells'
  own recorded scope (one device, one channel, the measured widths --
  and for :simdgroup-reduce its own flip cells only, W = 128, W = 256,
  W = 512 and
  W = 1024,
  n = 1000003)."
  ([vendor kir] (select-variant vendor kir nil nil))
  ([vendor kir target] (select-variant vendor kir target nil))
  ([vendor kir target device]
   ;; ADR-0356 rule 3 (emission admission first): a selected variant is one
   ;; the route's emitter can emit -- all four emitters admit
   ;; :subgroup-reduce since 2026-10-23 (the CUDA arm landed that day), and
   ;; the CUDA emitter additionally carries :warp-shuffle-reduce. Admission
   ;; is NECESSARY, not sufficient: the ROWS below decide each route's
   ;; answer, and a route outside every row answers :baseline.
   (let [route (emitter-route target)
         dev (or device {})
         ;; rule: validate the COMPLETE KIR first (a malformed kernel never
         ;; selects a variant).
         valid-kir? (try (accelerator/validate! kir) true (catch :default _ false))
         ;; ADR-0356 rule 6 (device capability): the NVIDIA row's sm_70+
         ;; floor is a real requirement; a descriptor that does not confirm
         ;; compute capability 7.0+ answers :baseline (fail closed, no
         ;; guessing).
         cuda-cap-ok? (let [cc (:compute-capability dev)]
                        (and (number? cc) (>= cc 7.0)))
         ;; ADR-0361: on the Vulkan route NVIDIA joins the shared
         ;; subgroup-reduce row, and the CUDA row's sm_70+ floor binds that
         ;; row whenever the descriptor reports a compute capability at all
         ;; -- a reported capability below 7.0, or a malformed one, fails
         ;; closed to :baseline, while an absent capability is not a
         ;; refusal (the rule-6 subgroup facts are the capability evidence
         ;; there, exactly as for the Intel/ARM/Apple rows).
         nvidia-floor-ok? (let [cc (:compute-capability dev)]
                            (or (nil? cc) (and (number? cc) (>= cc 7.0))))
         ;; ADR-0356 rule 6 and ADR-0357's ARM gate: the fold needs the
         ;; basic and shuffle-relative subgroup ops in the compute stage
         ;; (what the emission's two GL_KHR_shader_subgroup_* extensions
         ;; name) and a subgroup size that divides the workgroup. Every
         ;; missing or malformed fact fails closed to :baseline -- a
         ;; descriptor that reports no capability is not a refusal, it is an
         ;; absence of evidence, and the portable variant is the answer.
         subgroup-cap-ok?
         (let [s (:subgroup-size dev)
               ops (:subgroup-ops dev)
               wg (:kernel/workgroup-size kir)]
           (and (int? s) (pos? s)
                (set? ops)
                (contains? ops :basic)
                (contains? ops :shuffle-relative)
                (int? wg) (pos? wg)
                (zero? (mod wg s))))
         ;; ADR-0373 rule 3 step 2, rule 6 for the :simdgroup-reduce fold:
         ;; the gate's vocabulary grows ONE fact, :simd-reduce -- evidence
         ;; that the device's SIMD-group exposes simd_sum/simd_max/simd_min
         ;; -- plus the reported :subgroup-size (= SIMD-group width)
         ;; dividing W. :basic and :shuffle-relative are the SHUFFLE fold's
         ;; facts and are NOT required here (the cross-SIMD-group tree uses
         ;; threadgroup barriers, not subgroup operations). Every missing
         ;; or malformed fact fails closed, exactly like every other row.
         simd-reduce-cap-ok?
         (let [s (:subgroup-size dev)
               ops (:subgroup-ops dev)
               wg (:kernel/workgroup-size kir)]
           (and (int? s) (pos? s)
                (set? ops)
                (contains? ops :simd-reduce)
                (int? wg) (pos? wg)
                (zero? (mod wg s))))
         ;; ADR-0360's second decision (2026-10-23, JUN-58): the Apple
         ;; row is NARROWED to the measured device. A descriptor naming
         ;; the measured device (its name parts carry the fragment
         ;; "m1 max", uB5G idiom) answers :baseline for the reduce shape
         ;; on both Apple routes -- the row's own basis is refuted there
         ;; by hardware (the variant measured ~20-27% slower on the GLSL
         ;; arm and ~22% slower on the MSL arm). Every other Apple device
         ;; keeps the designed row (UNMEASURED).
         apple-narrowed-device?
         (and (= :apple vendor)
              (device-name-carries? dev apple-measured-fragment))
         subgroup-row-holds?
         (and (contains? #{:intel :arm :apple :nvidia :amd} vendor)
              (or (= :glsl-v1 route)
                  ;; ADR-0363: the WGSL route (:webgpu /
                  ;; :wgsl-v1) carries the Intel and ARM rows;
                  ;; ADR-0364 adds :amd (its vendor-default
                  ;; target, the ADR-0363 rationale).
                  (and (= :wgsl-v1 route)
                       (contains? #{:intel :arm :amd} vendor))
                  ;; ADR-0360's amendment (2026-10-23, JUN-58's
                  ;; own decision): the MSL route (:metal /
                  ;; :msl-v1) carries the Apple row, under the
                  ;; same rules 3 + 6 gate as the Vulkan route.
                  (and (= :msl-v1 route)
                       (= :apple vendor))
                  ;; ADR-0361's same-day amendment (JUN-57's own
                  ;; decision): the CUDA route (:cuda /
                  ;; :cuda-v1) carries the NVIDIA row as the
                  ;; SECOND rung of that route's ladder. The
                  ;; :warp-shuffle-reduce branch above is checked
                  ;; FIRST in this cond and keeps precedence
                  ;; whenever its gate holds (a CONFIRMED compute
                  ;; capability 7.0+ and W >= 32) -- every answer
                  ;; it gives is unchanged; this rung answers
                  ;; only where the warp row's gate does not
                  ;; reach (a descriptor reporting the rule-6
                  ;; subgroup facts and NO compute capability),
                  ;; under the same rules 3 + 6 gate, fail closed
                  ;; included.
                  (and (= :cuda-v1 route)
                       (= :nvidia vendor)))
              valid-kir?
              subgroup-cap-ok?
              ;; ADR-0361: NVIDIA's row carries the CUDA row's
              ;; sm_70+ floor; the other four vendors (AMD's
              ;; row, ADR-0364, included) have no
              ;; compute-capability contract and pass this gate
              ;; on the absent fact alone.
              (or (not= :nvidia vendor) nvidia-floor-ok?)
              (subgroup-reduce/applicable? kir))
         narrowed? (and subgroup-row-holds? apple-narrowed-device?)
         ;; ADR-0373 rule 3 step 2, the MSL ladder's FIRST rung: outside
         ;; the carved-out measured class, an Apple MSL descriptor that
         ;; reports the fold's rule-6 fact (:simd-reduce) and a reported
         ;; SIMD-group width dividing W answers :simdgroup-reduce for the
         ;; reduce shape. The row is Apple's alone (one vendor per
         ;; decision) and the MSL route's alone (rule 3: the emission is
         ;; admitted on :msl-v1 only). The carve-out is checked at the
         ;; cond below: the measured class reaches this rung only at this
         ;; variant's own flip cells (simd-flip?, below).
         simdgroup-row-holds?
         (and (= :apple vendor)
              (= :msl-v1 route)
              valid-kir?
              simd-reduce-cap-ok?
              (simdgroup/applicable? kir))
         ;; ADR-0373's flip contract applied to this variant's OWN
         ;; measured cells (2026-10-23/24, JUN-58): hardware measured THIS
         ;; variant faster than :baseline at W = 128, W = 256, W = 512 and
         ;; W = 1024
         ;; on the
         ;; measured class (apple-simdgroup-flip-note), so the carve-out
         ;; lifts exactly at those cells. The keys are the tested device class (the uB5G
         ;; name fragment AND the reported subgroup size 32), the
         ;; measured channel's route (the MSL route under either
         ;; spelling), this row's own rule-6 gate (the measurement ran
         ;; the intrinsic fold), and the workgroup widths the channel ran
         ;; for this variant. Everything else fails closed to the
         ;; carve-out.
         simd-flip?
         (and simdgroup-row-holds?
              apple-narrowed-device?
              (= :msl-v1 route)
              (= apple-measured-subgroup-size (:subgroup-size dev))
              (contains? apple-simdgroup-measured-workgroups
                         (:kernel/workgroup-size kir)))
         variant (cond
                   (and (= :nvidia vendor)
                        (= :cuda-v1 route)
                        valid-kir?
                        cuda-cap-ok?
                        (= :reduce (:kernel/op kir))
                        (contains? #{:sum :max :min} (:kernel/operator kir))
                        (let [wg (:kernel/workgroup-size kir)]
                          (and (int? wg) (>= wg min-warp-shuffle-workgroup))))
                   :warp-shuffle-reduce

                   ;; ADR-0373's measured-cell carve-out (measurement
                   ;; beats design) outranks rung 1 everywhere but this
                   ;; variant's OWN flip cells (simd-flip?): at W = 128,
                   ;; W = 256,
                   ;; W = 512 and W = 1024 on
                   ;; the measured class hardware measured THIS variant
                   ;; faster and the cells answer it, MEASURED. At every
                   ;; other W the narrowed M1 Max class keeps its
                   ;; reduce-shape answers. Rung 2 below
                   ;; is byte-for-byte the pre-step-2 answer.
                   (and simdgroup-row-holds?
                        (or (not apple-narrowed-device?) simd-flip?))
                   :simdgroup-reduce

                   (and subgroup-row-holds? (not narrowed?))
                   :subgroup-reduce

                   :else :baseline)
         ;; The carve-out's own note: it attaches only where rung 1's gate
         ;; held and the measured class refused the row -- the row's own
         ;; demotion, never a fail-closed :baseline, and never at this
         ;; variant's own flip cell (the measurement answered there, so
         ;; there is no demotion to annotate).
         carved? (and simdgroup-row-holds? apple-narrowed-device? (not simd-flip?))
         ;; ADR-0360 question (1)'s decision, Part 2 (2026-10-23, JUN-58):
         ;; the flip's record. A cell flips only when every key of the
         ;; tested device class holds -- the narrowed answer itself, the
         ;; measured device (the uB5G name fragment AND the reported
         ;; subgroup size 32 -- the contract keys on both), the measured
         ;; channel's route (the MSL route under either spelling; an MSL
         ;; measurement never flips a GLSL cell), and a workgroup width the
         ;; channel actually ran. The record names the class so no claim
         ;; extends past it; every other answer keeps :measured? false and
         ;; carries no record.
         ;; This variant's OWN flip records (the W = 512, W = 1024 and
         ;; W = 128
         ;; cells flipped in their own later slices): at each flip cell
         ;; the record names THIS variant's runs at that cell's own W and
         ;; its one measured shape -- the :subgroup-reduce runs say
         ;; nothing about this
         ;; variant (uB5G, reverse reading).
         simd-measured-cell
         (when simd-flip?
           {:device apple-measured-fragment
            :subgroup-size apple-measured-subgroup-size
            :channel apple-measured-channel
            :route :msl-v1
            :workgroup-size (:kernel/workgroup-size kir)
            :operators #{:sum :max :min}
            :shapes apple-simdgroup-measured-shapes
            :evidence (get apple-simdgroup-measured-evidence
                           (:kernel/workgroup-size kir))})
         measured-cell
         (or simd-measured-cell
             (when (and narrowed?
                        (= :msl-v1 route)
                        (= apple-measured-subgroup-size (:subgroup-size dev))
                        (contains? apple-measured-workgroups
                                   (:kernel/workgroup-size kir)))
               {:device apple-measured-fragment
                :subgroup-size apple-measured-subgroup-size
                :channel apple-measured-channel
                :route :msl-v1
                :workgroup-size (:kernel/workgroup-size kir)
                :operators #{:sum :max :min}
                :shapes apple-measured-shapes
                :evidence apple-measured-evidence}))]
    (cond-> {:variant variant
             :measured? (some? measured-cell)
             :basis (cond simd-flip?
                          (str (get variant-bases :simdgroup-reduce) "\n  "
                               apple-simdgroup-flip-note)
                          narrowed?
                          (str (get variant-bases :baseline) "\n  " apple-narrow-note
                               (when (= 32 (:kernel/workgroup-size kir))
                                 (str "\n  " apple-w32-note))
                               (when carved?
                                 (str "\n  " apple-simdgroup-carveout-note)))
                          carved?
                          (str (get variant-bases :baseline) "\n  "
                               apple-simdgroup-carveout-note)
                          :else (get variant-bases variant))}
      measured-cell (assoc :measured-cell measured-cell)))))
